            }
          ],
          "title": "Seaming",
          "slug": "seaming",
          "order": 2,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 4329,
            "blockType": "HTML"
          }
        },
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "Which comes first the chicken or the egg? <i class=\"fa fa-smile-o\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i><br>When creating this style of V-Neckband you have choices:<br>\r\n\r\n<ol>\r\n<li>Knit a band piece  and hang the neck opening on the needles and bind off loosely<br></li><br><li>Knit the band pieces, remove them on scrap.  Then rehang the neck edge, pull the band stitches through and loosely bind off.</li>\r\n<li>Hang the garment edges, knit a row and use a g-carriage (bind off edge will be public)</li>\r\n<li>Hang the garment edges, knit a row, transfer stitches to the ribber, hang weights and knit the ribbing (bind off edge will be public),</li>\r\n</ol>\r\n",
              "legacyComponentId": 6551,
              "order": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "video",
              "vimeoId": "202105212",
              "title": "Band Perfection",
              "legacyComponentId": 6552,
              "order": 2
            }
          ],
          "title": "Band Options",
          "slug": "band-options",
          "order": 4,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 4327,
            "blockType": "HTML"
          }
        },
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "Although the following video was created with raglan seams in mind, the decorative decreases can be put to use for V-neck shaping as well.<br>Make the decreases the focal point of your sweater.",
              "legacyComponentId": 6555,
              "order": 1
            },
            {
              "type": "video",
              "vimeoId": "151859783",
              "title": null,
              "legacyComponentId": 6553,
              "order": 2
            }
          ],
          "title": "Decorative Decrease Inspiration",
          "slug": "decorative-decrease-inspiration",
          "order": 5,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 4328,
            "blockType": "HTML"
          }
        },
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "exerciseAccordion",
              "sections": [
                {
                  "title": "Right and wrong side of cast on",
                  "bodyHtml": "\r\nDepending on the cast on method you choose, there may be a right and wrong side to the cast on edge. Since the cast on edge of the neck band is so visible, take time to determine which side looks best.\r\n\t<img src=\"/challenge/images/v2/89/compare_ribbing.png\">\r\n",
                  "iconSrc": "/challenge/images/v2/89/arrow1.png"
                },
                {
                  "title": "Protect your cast on edge",
                  "bodyHtml": "\r\n\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Work a zig zag row with waste yarn</li><br>\r\n<li>Hang the cast on comb in the waste yarn (this will protect your garment yarn edge)</li><br>\r\n<li>Switch to the garment yarn and knit another zig zag row</li><br>\r\n<li>Continue with your cast on</li><br>\r\n<li>When the knitting is complete, remove the comb and the ravel cord</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\t<img src=\"/challenge/images/v2/89/ravelcord2.jpg\">\r\n",
                  "iconSrc": "/challenge/images/v2/89/arrow2.png"
                },
                {
                  "title": "Our favorite cast on",
                  "bodyHtml": "Our favorite cast on is the \"broken toe\" cast on - it adds a strand of yarn that can be used to keep the edge from ruffling<br>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<a class='videopopup' data-vimeoid='151859232'><img class='img-thumbnail' src='https://i.vimeocdn.com/video/1212348843_1280x720?r=pad'>\r\n<div class=\"imagecenter text-center\"><button class=\"btn btn-success\"><i class=\"fa fa-youtube-play\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></button></div>\r\n</a>\r\n\r\n",
                  "iconSrc": "/challenge/images/v2/89/arrow3.png"
                },
                {
                  "title": "Check the row count (depth) of your ribbing.",
                  "bodyHtml": "If you've held 1\" of stitches at the bottom of the V - make sure you knit enough rows to make the band fit the opening.<br>You may not want to knit a swatch in ribbing, but make sure that you knit enough rows!",
                  "iconSrc": "/challenge/images/v2/89/arrow4.png"
                },
                {
                  "title": "Plan your needle arrangement",
                  "bodyHtml": "When you position your ribbing at the center front opening, you'll want to plan what stitch will be along the seamed edge. <Br>\r\n<img src=\"/challenge/images/v2/89/needle_arrangment.png\">",
                  "iconSrc": "/challenge/images/v2/89/arrow5.png"
                }
              ],
              "legacy": {
                "showText": "below",
                "size": null
              },
              "legacyComponentId": 6568,
              "order": 2
            }
          ],
          "title": "Band Tips",
          "slug": "band-tips",
          "order": 6,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 4335,
            "blockType": "HTML"
          }
        },
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "video",
              "vimeoId": "151859232",
              "title": "3 Perfect Ribbing Cast Ons",
              "legacyComponentId": 6567,
              "order": 1
            }
          ],
          "title": "Broken Toe Cast on",
          "slug": "broken-toe-cast-on",
          "order": 7,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 4336,
            "blockType": "HTML"
          }
        },
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "\r\nWhen dividing a neckline (V or round) you'll want to protect those stitches in hold from damage.  As you are knitting one side of the neckline, the carriage passes over the held stitches repeatedly and cause wear or create a dirty line in your knitting.<br>Over the years knitters, have devised a number of ways to protect those held stitches.\r\n<ul>\r\n<li>Scrap off the stitches </li>\r\n<li>Manually knit the held stitches with ravel cord, pulling them all the way back to \"A\" position</li>\r\n<li>Hang a rag over the stitches</li>\r\n<li>When you are ready to knit the 2nd half of the neck opening, remove the rag.  Voila! No damage!</li>\r\n\r\n</ul>\r\n<span class=\"handwriting\" style=\"font-size:2em\">Whatever method you use, make it a habit to protect those stitches!</span>\r\n<center>\r\n<figure>\r\n\r\n<img class=\"img-thumbnail\" src=\"/challenge/images/v2/89/protect_held_stitches.jpg\"><br>\r\n<figcaption><br>Rag over held needles</figcaption></figure></center>\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\t<center><img class=\"zoom\" src=\"/challenge/images/v2/89/short_row_damage_thumb.jpg\"><br>\r\nDamage/dirt from the carriage passing repeatedly over held stitches.\r\n</center>\r\n\r\n\r\n",
              "legacyComponentId": 6582,
              "order": 1
            }
          ],
          "title": "Protect Held Stitches",
          "slug": "protect-held-stitches",
          "order": 8,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 4345,
            "blockType": "HTML"
          }
        },
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "<style>\r\nhr.style13 {\r\nheight: 10px;\r\nborder: 0;\r\nbox-shadow: 0 10px 10px -10px #8c8b8b inset;\r\n}\r\n\r\n.container-border {\r\nborder: 1px solid #ccc;\r\nborder-radius: 15px;\r\npadding-top: 20px;\r\npadding-right: 20px;\r\npadding-bottom: 20px;\r\npadding-left: 20px;\r\n}\r\n\r\n</style>\r\n\r\n\r\nIf your pattern instructions or your Magic Formula results look like this, you may want to alternate your decreases for a straighter line.<br><img class=\"img-thumbnail\" src=\"/challenge/images/v2/89/dak99.jpg\"><br>In this example, decreases are worked every other row, then every 3rd row.  <mark>Notice that the shaping is not straight.</mark><br><img class=\"img-thumbnail\" src=\"/challenge/images/v2/89/dak6.png\">\r\n\r\n<hr class=\"style13\">\r\n\r\nMixing a few \"every 3rd row\" instructions can <mark>make the diagonal straight</mark>. <br><img class=\"img-thumbnail\" src=\"/challenge/images/v2/89/dak5.png\">\r\n\r\n<div class=\"container-border\"><span class=\"handwriting\" style=\"font-size:2em\">As long as you start and end with the correct number of stitches, you have permission to alternate the shaping as you see fit.</span><br><em>Just be sure to make both sides of your v-neck the same <i class=\"fa fa-smile-o fa-2x\" aria-hidden=\"true\"></i></em> </div>\r\n\r\n",
              "legacyComponentId": 6583,
              "order": 1
            }
          ],
          "title": "Even out the shaping",
          "slug": "even-out-the-shaping",
          "order": 9,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 4346,
            "blockType": "HTML"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "More Useful Skills",
      "slug": "more-useful-skills",
      "displayOrder": 6,
      "legacy": {
        "itemId": 549,
        "lessonOrder": 6
      },
      "blocks": [
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "\r\nEven for a sample exercise, you must accurately establish YOUR gauge. <br>You will be using the stockinette gauge to calculate the number of stitches and rows for your ribbed band.<br>If you choose to knit your sample in a stitch pattern for practice, you will need to swatch in both the stitch pattern and stockinette.<br><center></center>\r\n<center><a href=\"/classrooms/87/swatching-is-not-optional\"><img src=\"/path/images/87/swatch_thumb.jpg\"></a></center><br>Visit the Classroom: Swatching is NOT Optional\r\n\r\n\r\n",
              "legacyComponentId": 6550,
              "order": 1
            }
          ],
          "title": "Classroom: Swatching",
          "slug": "classroom-swatching",
          "order": 1,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 4326,
            "blockType": "HTML"
          }
        },
        {
          "components": [
            {
              "type": "richText",
              "html": "\r\nAnytime you modify a pattern in an area that is diagonal (sleeve shaping, shoulder shaping, waist shaping) you need to create knitting instructions to evenly place increases or decreases evenly along the knitting. <br>Use the Magic Formula (Diophantine dquation) for gradual shaping.<br><center></center>\r\n<center><a href=\"/classrooms/172/diagonals-magic-formula\"><img src=\"/path/images/172/diagonals_thumb.jpg\"></a><br>Visit the Classroom: Swatching is NOT Optional</center>\r\n\r\n\r\n",
              "legacyComponentId": 6562,
              "order": 1
            }
          ],
          "title": "Classroom: Diagonals and the Magic Formula",
          "slug": "classroom-diagonals-and-the-magic-formula",
          "order": 2,
          "legacy": {
            "assignId": 4332,
            "blockType": "HTML"
          }
        },
